From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:25:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: Do not keep media bodies that the HTTP cache cannot
 serve

Media elements fetch with `Range: bytes=N-`. The HTTP cache stored every
cacheable response to them that ran to its end, whatever its range or
size, as one more entry in the list of responses for the URL. A stored
partial response only answers a later range request that it covers, and
every load starts at byte 0, so the responses that a media backend
fetches from the middle of a resource, after a seek or to refill its
buffer, are rarely used. They stayed in memory, another copy on every
load, in a cache that is bounded by the number of URLs rather than by
size. Besides that, net kept the body of every response until its fetch
ended, so a long video sat whole in memory while it played.

For audio and video requests, store a 200 only if its Content-Length is
at most 8 MiB, and a 206 only if its Content-Range runs from byte 0 to
the end of a resource of at most 8 MiB and its Content-Length, if any, is
at most 8 MiB too; in both cases only without Content-Encoding, so that
this is the size of the body that net keeps. Other statuses, such as
redirects, follow the usual rules. A small video is then still answered
from the cache when the page is reloaded. The copies of a resource are
now bounded in size but not in number: a second element or a
revalidation can still add another copy of up to 8 MiB.

Do not keep the body of the other media responses at all. One function
decides both whether the cache may store a media response and whether
net keeps its body, so the cache never stores a body that was not kept.
Consumers get the body in chunks; the kept copy is only read by the
cache, by subresource integrity checks and by preloads, so it is still
kept for requests with integrity metadata and for preloads. Devtools
collects its own copy.

RFC 9111 never requires a cache to store a response, so this is a
storage decision that any cache may make, not a deviation.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: unit tests of the storage rules for media responses, and fetch
tests of a video of 8 MiB + 1 byte (neither kept nor stored) and of a
small one (answered from the cache on the second fetch).

Validation: Linux, headless release builds of ohos-main with the GStreamer backend, with and without this patch, playing a 90 s, 42 MB video from a local server with range support that sends ETag, Last-Modified and `Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400`; VmRSS of servoshell in MiB, two revisions of this patch that decide the same for all responses of these runs. Played once: 355 without, 314 and 329 with this patch. Played, reloaded and played again (after the first play / at the end): 351/442 and 356/455 without, 312/371 without this patch and with the cache disabled, 323/384 and 314/383 with this patch. A 2.8 MB video is answered from the cache after a reload if its first load ran to the end: in one of two runs the server got no request for it after the reload; in the other, the startup seek of GStreamer cancelled the first load. `./mach test-unit -p servo-net`: 395 tests pass; checked on the first revision, the new tests fail without the check in the cache (the large video is then stored with an empty body and served from it), and the fetch test of the large video fails when net keeps its body. `./mach test-wpt --release --processes 6 --timeout-multiplier 2` with both patches: fetch/http-cache, fetch/range, fetch/api/basic and xhr, all 619 tests as expected; html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements, run with the first revision of both patches, all as expected but four that behave the same with ohos-main when rerun three times alone: autoplay-default-permissions-policy.https.sub.html passes where a timeout is expected, media_fragment_seek.html fails a subtest every time, load-removes-queued-error-event.html crashes intermittently (1 of 3 runs with, 2 of 3 without the patches) and seek-to-max-value.htm passes every time.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; written, tested and measured by the agent, reworked after a review by another agent)
